+# Agent Note: Session-addressed cold preset selection
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-31-session-addressed-cold-preset-selection.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+The `agentPresets/select` Remote accepted an `Agent`, so API Gateway resolved its wire Session id through the shared Agent lookup before the target preset reached the selection service. Resolving a cold Session composes its recorded preset. A blank Session whose recorded preset had been removed or broken therefore could not select an available replacement, although the target composition and restored log were sufficient to decide the operation.
+
+Concurrent activation made the outcome timing-dependent. A valid selection that shared an unrelated resume failure inherited that failure instead of retrying with its own target.
+
+## Decision
+
+The wire operation remains `agentPresets/select(SessionId, presetId)`, but `SessionAgentPresets` in Session Controller owns that endpoint. The Agent Presets service owns the domain operations over a supplied live or unpublished Agent; its `select(Agent, presetId)` method is not a Remote lookup endpoint.
+
+Session Controller delegates a live ordinary Agent directly to the domain selection. For a cold ordinary Session, it observes the exact persisted log, prepares only the requested target composition on an unpublished Agent, publishes the Agent, and then appends `agent-preset/selected`. The restored log supplies the `turn/start` lock check. An unavailable target, subagent-owned identity, restore failure, or publication failure leaves the Agent unpublished and the durable selection unchanged.
+
+Cold selection shares an in-flight activation when it succeeds. If a shared activation fails for a reason that a target-specific activation can bypass, the waiting selection retries through its own target. The owner of a target-specific activation retains the stable preset-domain failure produced for that target.
+
+The generated Client namespace is assembled from two contributions: Agent Presets supplies roster and authoring methods, while Session Controller supplies `select`. Gateway and Client registries merge distinct methods under one namespace and reject duplicate endpoints.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Add request context to every Typert lookup resolver.** Rejected because this one operation needs both the raw Session identity and target preset, while extending the lookup protocol would change every provider and retain selection policy in a generic identity resolver.
+
+**Make the shared Agent lookup skip recorded composition.** Rejected because all other Agent-addressed operations require a fully composed live Agent and depend on strict cold-resume behavior.
+
+**Rewrite the Session creation header.** Rejected because the append-only selection event and its projection already own composition changes after creation; mutating the header would introduce a second durable authority.
+
+**Record the selection before Agent publication.** Rejected because a later synchronous creation listener can veto publication after Session persistence has observed the event, leaving a failed operation in the durable log.
+
+## Consequences
+
+A blank persisted Session can recover from a deleted or broken recorded preset by choosing an available target. The target composition exists before any Agent publication notification, while the selection event is appended only after the complete publication sequence succeeds. Session and Agent creation listeners can therefore observe the target composition before the corresponding selection event is appended; callers cannot observe a successful selection response until both publication and event append complete.
+
+Focused tests cover live and cold selection, restored turn locking, invalid targets without publication or events, concurrent target failures, and takeover after an unrelated recorded-preset resume fails. Generated Host descriptors keep the raw `SessionId` as a JSON parameter and the Client type merges `select` with the other `agentPresets` methods.
